The service uses one ReentrantLock around each post lookup and state operation.
Lock acquisition order is the authoritative commit order, ID allocation is atomic,
and readers receive a consistent snapshot. DTOs are deep copies, so caller changes
never mutate stored objects.

For higher throughput, locks could be partitioned by post while global IDs use
atomic counters. Horizontal scaling requires shared durable storage; database
transactions would then define commit order and snapshot semantics across instances.
